<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Troezen</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Troezen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small city of the eastern Argolid, mythical birthplace of Theseus, which sheltered Athenian families during Xerxes's invasion of 480 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Troezen lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a> peninsula facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saronic-gulf">Saronic Gulf</a>, with Ionian traditions shared with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and later Dorian settlement. Myth made it the birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a> and the home of his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittheus">Pittheus</a>. In the Archaic period it was a small independent city with a harbour at Pogon.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Troezenians helped found <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/halicarnassus">Halicarnassus</a> and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaeans-achaea">Achaeans</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sybaris">Sybaris</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In 480 BCE Athenian women and children were evacuated to Troezen before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Salamis</a>, as recorded in the inscribed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troezen-decree">Troezen Decree</a> found in 1959. Troezen fought at Salamis and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/plataea">Plataea</a> and was usually a member of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peloponnesian-league">Peloponnesian League</a>. It had a sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippolytus-son-of-theseus">Hippolytus</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> Troezen changed hands among Macedonian kings and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> before joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League</a>. It passed under Roman control in 146 BCE and continued as a small town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/troezen.jpg" alt="Troezen" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Schuppi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troizina_Village_Main_Square.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Troezen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cults of Hippolytus and Poseidon</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Troizen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=troezen" aria-label="Troezen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5264 828 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Troezen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5584 1084L5562 1098M5584 1084L5549 1060"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5562 1098h0M5549 1060h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5584 1084h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5584 1084h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=troezen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

